{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["The simple sense of <b>your burden and your load and your strife</b>: Moses our Teacher hinted to them the three things that he told Jethro he himself was doing for the people, as I explained there (Exodus 18:15). And he mentioned them to Israel in a hint: he said <b>your burden</b> corresponding to \"and I shall make known the statutes of Elohim and His laws\" (Exodus 18:16), for it was a great toil to teach the statutes and laws and their interpretations and explanations and their secrets to those who came out of Egypt. And he mentioned <b>your load</b> corresponding to \"to inquire of Elohim\" (Exodus 18:15), which is the matter of prayer, that he would pray on their behalf — and it is from the language of \"and you shall bear a prayer\" (II Kings 19:4), and \"do not bear a cry and prayer on their behalf\" (Jeremiah 7:16). <b>And your strife</b> is as its simple meaning — matters of judgment.\n\nAnd he said, \"Provide for yourselves men who are wise, understanding, and knowledgeable\" concerning the judges alone, but he said merely <b>and I shall set them at your head</b> out of humility.\n\nAnd the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 1:12), from Sifrei (Deuteronomy 12): <b>your burden</b> teaches that they were contentious — if one of them saw his adversary winning the case, he would say, \"I have witnesses to bring, I have proofs to bring,\" \"I am adding judges over you.\" This law I do not know, that a litigant should be able to add judges beyond three in monetary cases, and all the more so after he has pleaded before them and seen that his adversary is winning the case — for if he had accepted upon himself a relative or a disqualified judge, he can retract until after the conclusion of judgment, but as for qualified judges, he cannot retract. And perhaps we may learn from here that a person may say, \"I select two judges for myself, and you select two others, and they shall select yet another,\" and the judgment may be concluded with five or more, as they said: \"A judgment concluded with five is not like a judgment concluded with three.\" And even though three may force him to be judged before them when he does not wish to accept judgment upon himself, nevertheless, one who wishes to select more may select, for that would be like saying, \"Let us go to the place of the assembly,\" where they listen to him in a place where there is no burden — for in this we were commanded, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 16:20), \"Justice, justice you shall pursue\" — go after a fine court (Sanhedrin 32b), and the presence of many sages in a fine court is a virtue. And behold, in the wilderness the wise men were present among them, and they could say, \"Let us go before the officers of thousands.\""], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:15.465455633Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}